{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVZh7u2GqJNLKYVktKVM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've pulled in three more references to fill the remaining gaps—Freund & Hopkins (2023) on robustness auditing, Agarwal et al. (2020) on rank aggregation under corruption, and Cattelan, Varin & Firth (2013) on dynamic Bradley–Terry models—bringing the bibliography to the 30-reference cap. Now I'll weave them into the anchored paragraphs.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":718,"cache_read_input_tokens":58737,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":718},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e124b283-7beb-4c0b-bb14-027f4e696ac4","uuid":"8c4bbf25-7455-4d19-bfe7-161799b24d84","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:15:20.529Z","request_id":"req_011CgVZh7HpKz4ZhacZJbQfY","narration_block_indexes":[0]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVZh7u2GqJNLKYVktKVM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R91sjVxiTd1BXnQn34hpAq","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n -i -E \"combinatorial search|Provably|Upper bounds, not minima|attacker and aggregator|FiveThirtyEight|Elo1978|cite\\{Elo|Moitra\" paper.tex; grep -n \"^@\" references.bib | head -40","description":"Locate anchor sentences in paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":718,"cache_read_input_tokens":58737,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":718},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e124b283-7beb-4c0b-bb14-027f4e696ac4","uuid":"b8c5f23b-17ec-4d91-a2fd-127fe7d5b40d","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:15:20.537Z","request_id":"req_011CgVZh7HpKz4ZhacZJbQfY"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R91sjVxiTd1BXnQn34hpAq","type":"tool_result","content":"48:Pairwise-comparison leaderboards have become a central instrument of model evaluation. Chatbot Arena aggregates crowdsourced votes between two anonymous chatbots~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}. MT-Bench collects expert and GPT-4 judgments on a fixed question set~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}. Sports ratings descend from the same paired-comparison tradition~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. In each case a Bradley--Terry (BT) model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} turns the comparisons into scores, and the scores into a ranking. Rankings are then used to choose models, to advertise releases, and to compare research systems. Their confidence intervals usually come from resampling or asymptotic arguments~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA,Gao2021UncertaintyQI}. These describe \\emph{random} variation, not how much \\emph{specific} data the conclusion rests on. Recent work shows that crowdsourced leaderboards can be moved deliberately~\\citep{Min2025ImprovingYM}, that evaluation practice shapes them~\\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and that dropping a small number of preferences can alter top Arena rankings~\\citep{Huang2025DroppingJA}. What remains open is how this sensitivity \\emph{compares across evaluation designs}, and how the answer depends on what is measured.\n73:The BT model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} and its extensions to ties~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET} underlie most comparison leaderboards. Ties are often handled, as here, by counting them as half wins. Maximum-likelihood fitting is classical~\\citep{Hunter2003MMAF}. Spectral and counting alternatives come with sample-complexity guarantees for recovering the top-$K$ items~\\citep{Negahban2012RankCR,Chen2015SpectralMT,Chen2017SPECTRALMA,Shah2015SimpleRA}. Elo ratings play the same role in sports and games~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. Uncertainty for BT scores is usually quantified by asymptotic theory~\\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI} or the bootstrap~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA}, and the robustness of Elo-style rankings of language models has itself been examined~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}. These tools answer ``how variable is the ranking under resampling?''. We ask a worst-case, data-specific question: ``how few concrete comparisons does the ranking rest on?''.\n76:Influence diagnostics go back to Cook's distance~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI}. They were extended to nonlinear models~\\citep{Monari2000WithdrawingAE} and to modern machine learning~\\citep{Koh2017UnderstandingBP}, though they can be fragile for deep networks~\\citep{Basu2020InfluenceFI}. Infinitesimal-jackknife approximations make leave-out analysis cheap~\\citep{Giordano2018ASA}, and higher-order influence expansions refine it~\\citep{Giordano2019AHS,Alaa2020DiscriminativeJQ}. \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF} turn this machinery into a robustness metric, the approximate maximum influence perturbation: the smallest fraction of data whose removal can overturn a conclusion. For ordinary least squares in low dimensions, such drop-data conclusions can be audited with provable guarantees~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We adopt the drop-data question, apply it to rank conclusions of a BT fit, add an outcome-reversal perturbation, and certify every size by exact refits rather than reporting the linear approximation.\n79:Chatbot Arena~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA} and MT-Bench~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW} established crowdsourced and judge-based pairwise evaluation of chat models. Automatic judges have known biases, such as a preference for length, which can be partly corrected~\\citep{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA}. \\citet{Min2025ImprovingYM} show that a model's Arena ranking can be improved by rigging votes, and \\citet{Singh2025TheLI} document how private testing and unequal data access distort the Arena leaderboard. \\citet{Huang2025DroppingJA} report that dropping a handful of preferences can change top LLM rankings. Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator~\\citep{Ma2022ATO}. We do not claim to be the first to show that an Arena leader is fragile. Our contribution is the controlled comparison across crowdsourced, expert, LLM-judge, and sports designs under one estimator and protocol. We also contrast drop and flip perturbations and top-1 and top-$k$ targets, and we show that cross-design conclusions depend on the size scale.\n117:$\\kver$ is an \\emph{upper bound} on $k^\\star$: an explicit set of $\\kver$ rows exists whose perturbation changes the target under the exact estimator. Nothing smaller is ruled out. Computing $k^\\star$ is a combinatorial search problem. Provable auditing is known only in special settings such as low-dimensional least squares~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We do not claim to solve it on real data. Instead, we check on a planted case with a known exact minimum that the instrument is not loose (below).\n126:Table~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. The four designs are crowdsourced votes from the Chatbot Arena 140k human-preference release~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}; expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments of MT-Bench answers~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}; and NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record, the 2014 and 2015 seasons in that file's labeling, with each game a comparison between two teams. Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties. Each design contributes three cases: drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$. With the two controls this gives 17 cases. All method choices were made on disjoint development data: the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. Any final-split row whose content hash also appears there is excluded; the recorded cross-split overlap is zero. The 17 cases were then run once in a sealed evaluation under seeds 101, 102, and 103. Every episode either returns a set that the evaluator verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed. The evaluation completed and passed all of its validity gates.\n142:Sports & NBA, 2014 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}319 & 5 & one game \\\\\n143:Sports & NBA, 2015 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}311 & 5 & one game \\\\\n253:\\paragraph{Upper bounds, not minima.}\n363:All data are public. Arena: \\nolinkurl{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\nolinkurl{lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\nolinkurl{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\nolinkurl{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season. Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\nolinkurl{task/resources/final/split_manifest.json}.\n1:@Inproceedings{Broderick2020AnAF,\n7:@Article{Giordano2019AHS,\n16:@Article{Monari2000WithdrawingAE,\n26:@Article{Alaa2020DiscriminativeJQ,\n35:@Article{Bradley1952RankAO,\n44:@Article{Zheng2023JudgingLW,\n53:@Article{Chiang2024ChatbotAA,\n61:@Article{Koh2017UnderstandingBP,\n69:@Article{Min2025ImprovingYM,\n78:@Article{Singh2025TheLI,\n87:@Article{Huang2025DroppingJA,\n96:@Article{Chen2015SpectralMT,\n104:@Article{Negahban2012RankCR,\n114:@Article{Hunter2003MMAF,\n123:@Article{Giordano2018ASA,\n131:@Inproceedings{Elo1978TheRO,\n137:@Article{Gao2021UncertaintyQI,\n145:@Article{Chen2017SPECTRALMA,\n155:@Article{Cook2000DetectionOI,\n165:@Article{Davidson1970OnET,\n174:@Article{Ma2022ATO,\n184:@Article{Moitra2022ProvablyAO,\n193:@Article{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA,\n202:@Article{Boubdir2023EloUR,\n211:@Article{Efron1979BootstrapMA,\n220:@Article{Shah2015SimpleRA,\n229:@Article{Basu2020InfluenceFI,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e124b283-7beb-4c0b-bb14-027f4e696ac4","uuid":"de470f79-7696-4a30-b36b-9f41304a73ee","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:15:20.608Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"48:Pairwise-comparison leaderboards have become a central instrument of model evaluation. Chatbot Arena aggregates crowdsourced votes between two anonymous chatbots~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}. MT-Bench collects expert and GPT-4 judgments on a fixed question set~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}. Sports ratings descend from the same paired-comparison tradition~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. In each case a Bradley--Terry (BT) model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} turns the comparisons into scores, and the scores into a ranking. Rankings are then used to choose models, to advertise releases, and to compare research systems. Their confidence intervals usually come from resampling or asymptotic arguments~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA,Gao2021UncertaintyQI}. These describe \\emph{random} variation, not how much \\emph{specific} data the conclusion rests on. Recent work shows that crowdsourced leaderboards can be moved deliberately~\\citep{Min2025ImprovingYM}, that evaluation practice shapes them~\\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and that dropping a small number of preferences can alter top Arena rankings~\\citep{Huang2025DroppingJA}. What remains open is how this sensitivity \\emph{compares across evaluation designs}, and how the answer depends on what is measured.\n73:The BT model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} and its extensions to ties~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET} underlie most comparison leaderboards. Ties are often handled, as here, by counting them as half wins. Maximum-likelihood fitting is classical~\\citep{Hunter2003MMAF}. Spectral and counting alternatives come with sample-complexity guarantees for recovering the top-$K$ items~\\citep{Negahban2012RankCR,Chen2015SpectralMT,Chen2017SPECTRALMA,Shah2015SimpleRA}. Elo ratings play the same role in sports and games~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. Uncertainty for BT scores is usually quantified by asymptotic theory~\\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI} or the bootstrap~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA}, and the robustness of Elo-style rankings of language models has itself been examined~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}. These tools answer ``how variable is the ranking under resampling?''. We ask a worst-case, data-specific question: ``how few concrete comparisons does the ranking rest on?''.\n76:Influence diagnostics go back to Cook's distance~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI}. They were extended to nonlinear models~\\citep{Monari2000WithdrawingAE} and to modern machine learning~\\citep{Koh2017UnderstandingBP}, though they can be fragile for deep networks~\\citep{Basu2020InfluenceFI}. Infinitesimal-jackknife approximations make leave-out analysis cheap~\\citep{Giordano2018ASA}, and higher-order influence expansions refine it~\\citep{Giordano2019AHS,Alaa2020DiscriminativeJQ}. \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF} turn this machinery into a robustness metric, the approximate maximum influence perturbation: the smallest fraction of data whose removal can overturn a conclusion. For ordinary least squares in low dimensions, such drop-data conclusions can be audited with provable guarantees~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We adopt the drop-data question, apply it to rank conclusions of a BT fit, add an outcome-reversal perturbation, and certify every size by exact refits rather than reporting the linear approximation.\n79:Chatbot Arena~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA} and MT-Bench~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW} established crowdsourced and judge-based pairwise evaluation of chat models. Automatic judges have known biases, such as a preference for length, which can be partly corrected~\\citep{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA}. \\citet{Min2025ImprovingYM} show that a model's Arena ranking can be improved by rigging votes, and \\citet{Singh2025TheLI} document how private testing and unequal data access distort the Arena leaderboard. \\citet{Huang2025DroppingJA} report that dropping a handful of preferences can change top LLM rankings. Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator~\\citep{Ma2022ATO}. We do not claim to be the first to show that an Arena leader is fragile. Our contribution is the controlled comparison across crowdsourced, expert, LLM-judge, and sports designs under one estimator and protocol. We also contrast drop and flip perturbations and top-1 and top-$k$ targets, and we show that cross-design conclusions depend on the size scale.\n117:$\\kver$ is an \\emph{upper bound} on $k^\\star$: an explicit set of $\\kver$ rows exists whose perturbation changes the target under the exact estimator. Nothing smaller is ruled out. Computing $k^\\star$ is a combinatorial search problem. Provable auditing is known only in special settings such as low-dimensional least squares~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We do not claim to solve it on real data. Instead, we check on a planted case with a known exact minimum that the instrument is not loose (below).\n126:Table~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. The four designs are crowdsourced votes from the Chatbot Arena 140k human-preference release~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}; expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments of MT-Bench answers~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}; and NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record, the 2014 and 2015 seasons in that file's labeling, with each game a comparison between two teams. Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties. Each design contributes three cases: drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$. With the two controls this gives 17 cases. All method choices were made on disjoint development data: the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. Any final-split row whose content hash also appears there is excluded; the recorded cross-split overlap is zero. The 17 cases were then run once in a sealed evaluation under seeds 101, 102, and 103. Every episode either returns a set that the evaluator verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed. The evaluation completed and passed all of its validity gates.\n142:Sports & NBA, 2014 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}319 & 5 & one game \\\\\n143:Sports & NBA, 2015 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}311 & 5 & one game \\\\\n253:\\paragraph{Upper bounds, not minima.}\n363:All data are public. Arena: \\nolinkurl{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\nolinkurl{lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\nolinkurl{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\nolinkurl{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season. Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\nolinkurl{task/resources/final/split_manifest.json}.\n1:@Inproceedings{Broderick2020AnAF,\n7:@Article{Giordano2019AHS,\n16:@Article{Monari2000WithdrawingAE,\n26:@Article{Alaa2020DiscriminativeJQ,\n35:@Article{Bradley1952RankAO,\n44:@Article{Zheng2023JudgingLW,\n53:@Article{Chiang2024ChatbotAA,\n61:@Article{Koh2017UnderstandingBP,\n69:@Article{Min2025ImprovingYM,\n78:@Article{Singh2025TheLI,\n87:@Article{Huang2025DroppingJA,\n96:@Article{Chen2015SpectralMT,\n104:@Article{Negahban2012RankCR,\n114:@Article{Hunter2003MMAF,\n123:@Article{Giordano2018ASA,\n131:@Inproceedings{Elo1978TheRO,\n137:@Article{Gao2021UncertaintyQI,\n145:@Article{Chen2017SPECTRALMA,\n155:@Article{Cook2000DetectionOI,\n165:@Article{Davidson1970OnET,\n174:@Article{Ma2022ATO,\n184:@Article{Moitra2022ProvablyAO,\n193:@Article{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA,\n202:@Article{Boubdir2023EloUR,\n211:@Article{Efron1979BootstrapMA,\n220:@Article{Shah2015SimpleRA,\n229:@Article{Basu2020InfluenceFI,","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
