{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVZheqrvEp8DdfuQgp7Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CKFtynXLP4JHLW8S189bou","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 250,258p paper.tex; sed -n 87,95p references.bib; sed -n 184,192p references.bib","description":"Read limitation paragraph and bib entry formats"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3829,"cache_read_input_tokens":59455,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3829},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e124b283-7beb-4c0b-bb14-027f4e696ac4","uuid":"94a68f1e-d7a7-45a1-9283-29077c194c9a","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:15:24.968Z","request_id":"req_011CgVZheCurQRvStNYJeYhQ"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CKFtynXLP4JHLW8S189bou","type":"tool_result","content":"For one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n\n\\paragraph{Upper bounds, not minima.}\nEvery size is an upper bound $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$. Exactness is demonstrated only on the three-player planted case. On real data the true minimum may be smaller, possibly by a lot for cases with many players. Statements such as ``the leader rests on 62 votes'' should be read as ``at most 62 votes''.\n\n\\paragraph{No sampling uncertainty on $\\kver$.}\nThe three seeds give identical sizes because the finder is deterministic. They do not quantify how $\\kver$ would vary under resampling of comparisons or under a different data snapshot. The bootstrap intervals in Table~\\ref{tab:validity} are over cases, for comparator ratios, not over data. The random-comparator summary is censored at the clip of $\\log_2=4$.\n\n@Article{Huang2025DroppingJA,\n author = {Jenny Y. Huang and Yunyi Shen and Dennis Wei and Tamara Broderick},\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Dropping Just a Handful of Preferences Can Change Top Large Language Model Rankings},\n volume = {abs/2508.11847},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Article{Moitra2022ProvablyAO,\n author = {Ankur Moitra and Dhruv Rohatgi},\n booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Provably Auditing Ordinary Least Squares in Low Dimensions},\n volume = {abs/2205.14284},\n year = {2022}\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e124b283-7beb-4c0b-bb14-027f4e696ac4","uuid":"d43ff60c-687e-401f-bfcf-40249fda0d36","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:15:25.054Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"For one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n\n\\paragraph{Upper bounds, not minima.}\nEvery size is an upper bound $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$. Exactness is demonstrated only on the three-player planted case. On real data the true minimum may be smaller, possibly by a lot for cases with many players. Statements such as ``the leader rests on 62 votes'' should be read as ``at most 62 votes''.\n\n\\paragraph{No sampling uncertainty on $\\kver$.}\nThe three seeds give identical sizes because the finder is deterministic. They do not quantify how $\\kver$ would vary under resampling of comparisons or under a different data snapshot. The bootstrap intervals in Table~\\ref{tab:validity} are over cases, for comparator ratios, not over data. The random-comparator summary is censored at the clip of $\\log_2=4$.\n\n@Article{Huang2025DroppingJA,\n author = {Jenny Y. Huang and Yunyi Shen and Dennis Wei and Tamara Broderick},\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Dropping Just a Handful of Preferences Can Change Top Large Language Model Rankings},\n volume = {abs/2508.11847},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Article{Moitra2022ProvablyAO,\n author = {Ankur Moitra and Dhruv Rohatgi},\n booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Provably Auditing Ordinary Least Squares in Low Dimensions},\n volume = {abs/2205.14284},\n year = {2022}\n}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
