{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVZj89xW9hiT9EKrwJEG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01G4K2pV9yWhY8eJYYQjMenw","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n -i \"ridge\\|lambda\" paper.tex | head -8; grep -rn -i \"time\\|date\\|dynamic\" source_evidence --include=*.py | grep -v -i \"timeout\\|perf_counter\\|deadline\\|time()\" | head","description":"Confirm the estimator is a static BT fit"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1409,"cache_read_input_tokens":65349,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1409},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e124b283-7beb-4c0b-bb14-027f4e696ac4","uuid":"bc9717a6-22dd-4d31-905d-ba5ee90fc34d","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:15:45.282Z","request_id":"req_011CgVZj7bESe6mu76UJW6ad"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G4K2pV9yWhY8eJYYQjMenw","type":"tool_result","content":"38:Leaderboards for language models and sports rank participants by fitting a Bradley--Terry (BT) model to pairwise comparisons. It is rarely measured how much of the comparison data a reported ranking depends on. We measure the smallest perturbation we can find and certify that changes the leader or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard. A perturbation either drops comparisons or reverses their outcomes. The search orders comparisons by their first-order influence on the decisive score margin, and an exact refit verifies every reported size. We evaluate 15 cases spanning crowdsourced votes (Chatbot Arena), expert and GPT-4-judge annotations (MT-Bench), and two NBA seasons. In every design, tens of comparisons change the leader: 62 of 135,622 Arena votes (0.046\\%), 92 expert and 143 GPT-4 judgments, and 7 and 16 NBA games. These sets are about $16\\times$ smaller than random perturbations, and identical across seeds. The most fragile design depends on the scale and the target. As a fraction of the data Arena is the most fragile; by count, NBA is. Moving a top-$k$ boundary can cost one game, or more than changing the leader. All sizes are upper bounds; cross-design differences are descriptive, not causal.\n50:We ask: \\emph{how small a verified perturbation of the comparison data changes the top-1 or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard? How does that size differ between crowdsourced votes, expert annotation, an LLM judge, and sports results?} We answer with a measurement instrument, not a new estimator. Following the drop-data robustness metric of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}, we order comparisons by a one-step influence approximation~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP} of their effect on the decisive score margin. We find the shortest prefix of that ordering whose removal (or outcome reversal) changes the target. An exact refit checks every candidate. Each reported size is therefore a certified \\emph{upper bound} on the minimal perturbation, not an approximation.\n65:\\caption{Protocol overview. Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fit with a ridge Bradley--Terry model. Rows are ordered by their one-step influence on the decisive margin: leader vs.\\ runner-up for top-1, or the $k$-th vs.\\ $(k{+}1)$-th player for a top-$k$ set. The shortest prefix whose removal or outcome reversal changes the target is found by exponential and binary search. Every probe is an exact refit, and the final set is re-verified independently. Sizes are reported as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$. A random-subset comparator, a direct leader--challenger heuristic, a planted-minimum control, a row-permutation control, and seed replay calibrate the instrument.}\n85:A case consists of $n$ comparison rows $r=(a_r,b_r,y_r)$ among $p$ players. The outcome is $y_r\\in\\{1,\\tfrac12,0\\}$: a win for $a_r$, a tie, or a win for $b_r$. The leaderboard is the ridge-penalized BT maximum-likelihood estimate\n87:\\hat\\theta=\\arg\\max_{\\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^p}\\;\\sum_{r=1}^{n}\\Big[y_r\\log\\sigma(\\theta_{a_r}-\\theta_{b_r})+(1-y_r)\\log\\sigma(\\theta_{b_r}-\\theta_{a_r})\\Big]-\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\theta\\|_2^2 ,\n90:where $\\sigma$ is the logistic function, ties count as half a win for each side, and $\\lambda=0.1$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. Players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. After that the player set is fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The estimator constants are fixed in the evaluator (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 129--135), and the finder's own refit mirrors them (source locations in Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}).\n105:Let $L$ and $C$ be the $k$-th and $(k{+}1)$-th players under $\\hat\\theta$; for top-1 these are the leader and the runner-up. Let $m=\\hat\\theta_L-\\hat\\theta_C>0$ be the decisive margin and $c=e_L-e_C$. The negative Hessian of \\eqref{eq:bt} at $\\hat\\theta$ is $H=\\sum_r p_r(1-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})^\\top+\\lambda I$, with $p_r=\\sigma(\\hat\\theta_{a_r}-\\hat\\theta_{b_r})$. Row $r$ contributes the gradient $(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$. Removing it moves the optimum by approximately $-H^{-1}(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP}. With $v=H^{-1}c$, the first-order effects of dropping or flipping row $r$ on the margin are therefore\n260:All results use ridge BT with $\\lambda=0.1$, ties counted as half wins, and a 50-comparison minimum per player. Other choices could change sizes, sometimes qualitatively. Examples include Elo-style online updates~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}, a Davidson tie model~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, a different penalty, or style-controlled scores. Each design is represented by a single snapshot, and sports by two seasons. We have no within-design replication across releases.\nsource_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py:4:candidate runs isolated (no files, no network, no shared filesystem) and keeps all state in\nsource_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py:19:   \"player_b\": int32[m], \"outcome\": float32[m]}``   (sent n_chunks times, chunk_index 0..n-1)\nsource_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py:23:   text, dates, labels or scores are ever sent.  Response: exactly ``{\"ok\": true}``.\nsource_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py:49:import time\nsource_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py:152:    started = time.monotonic()\nsource_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py:197:    while n > 0 and time.monotonic() - started < budget:\nsource_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py:208:        while hi - lo > 1 and time.monotonic() - started < budget:\nsource_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py:4:candidate runs isolated (no files, no network, no shared filesystem) and keeps all state in\nsource_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py:19:   \"player_b\": int32[m], \"outcome\": float32[m]}``   (sent n_chunks times, chunk_index 0..n-1)\nsource_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py:23:   text, dates, labels or scores are ever sent.  Response: exactly ``{\"ok\": true}``.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e124b283-7beb-4c0b-bb14-027f4e696ac4","uuid":"edc87651-396a-4ba7-913d-2abb71f6d041","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:15:45.363Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"38:Leaderboards for language models and sports rank participants by fitting a Bradley--Terry (BT) model to pairwise comparisons. It is rarely measured how much of the comparison data a reported ranking depends on. We measure the smallest perturbation we can find and certify that changes the leader or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard. A perturbation either drops comparisons or reverses their outcomes. The search orders comparisons by their first-order influence on the decisive score margin, and an exact refit verifies every reported size. We evaluate 15 cases spanning crowdsourced votes (Chatbot Arena), expert and GPT-4-judge annotations (MT-Bench), and two NBA seasons. In every design, tens of comparisons change the leader: 62 of 135,622 Arena votes (0.046\\%), 92 expert and 143 GPT-4 judgments, and 7 and 16 NBA games. These sets are about $16\\times$ smaller than random perturbations, and identical across seeds. The most fragile design depends on the scale and the target. As a fraction of the data Arena is the most fragile; by count, NBA is. Moving a top-$k$ boundary can cost one game, or more than changing the leader. All sizes are upper bounds; cross-de