|_2^2 ,\n90:where $\\sigma$ is the logistic function, ties count as half a win for each side, and $\\lambda=0.1$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. Players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. After that the player set is fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The estimator constants are fixed in the evaluator (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 129--135), and the finder's own refit mirrors them (source locations in Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}).\n105:Let $L$ and $C$ be the $k$-th and $(k{+}1)$-th players under $\\hat\\theta$; for top-1 these are the leader and the runner-up. Let $m=\\hat\\theta_L-\\hat\\theta_C>0$ be the decisive margin and $c=e_L-e_C$. The negative Hessian of \\eqref{eq:bt} at $\\hat\\theta$ is $H=\\sum_r p_r(1-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})^\\top+\\lambda I$, with $p_r=\\sigma(\\hat\\theta_{a_r}-\\hat\\theta_{b_r})$. Row $r$ contributes the gradient $(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$. Removing it moves the optimum by approximately $-H^{-1}(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP}. With $v=H^{-1}c$, the first-order effects of dropping or flipping row $r$ on the margin are therefore\n260:All results use ridge BT with $\\lambda=0.1$, ties counted as half wins, and a 50-comparison minimum per player. Other choices could change sizes, sometimes qualitatively. Examples include Elo-style online updates~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}, a Davidson tie model~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, a different penalty, or style-controlled scores. Each design is represented by a single snapshot, and sports by two seasons. We have no within-design repl