--- final message ---
I filled all three citation gaps in the draft. The rest of the paper is unchanged, and all checks pass except the compile script's built-in check, which this environment can't run (see the last section).

**Why I fetched references myself:** the automatic post-draft retrieval found 25 candidates, but none were added: `references.bib` was byte-identical to the version before the draft. So I fetched three specific papers through the reference-retrieval script and recorded each in `references_provenance.json`. That brings the bibliography to 30 entries, the maximum allowed.

**Edits to `paper.tex`, at the flagged sentences only:**
1. **Sizes are upper bounds** (Method paragraph "What a verified size means" and the Discussion paragraph "Upper bounds, not minima"): added Freund & Hopkins (2023). It shows that exact search, and proving no smaller set exists, get computationally hard beyond a few dimensions even for least-squares regression, while greedy or influence-based methods only produce upper bounds. The limitation paragraph now cites this together with Moitra & Rohatgi (2022) and notes that a Bradley–Terry fit with tens of players is far outside that setting.
2. **Related Work, attacks on rank aggregation:** added Agarwal et al. (ICML 2020) on ranking from pairwise comparisons when an adversary corrupts a fraction of them. This is the setting the paper's outcome-reversal ("flip") perturbation tests on real data. The search tool returned the wrong author order, so I corrected it from the DBLP record.
3. **NBA data paragraph:** added Cattelan, Varin & Firth (2013) on Bradley–Terry models for sports tournaments. I also added one sentence saying that each season gets a static fit with no time-varying team strength. The code confirms this: the program that searches for perturbations never receives game dates.

I added three non-numerical entries to `claims_grounding.json`, for 24 in total.

**Not added:** a 2024 paper on failure modes of approximate data dropping would have fit gap 1 well. I left it out because it is by the same group as a paper the draft already cites, and its 2025 journal version may discuss the line of work the task forbids reading.

**Checks:**
- Claims validator: 24 claims and 30 references pass.
- Citation-key check: all 30 keys resolve.
- Evidence verifier: 0 dangling references.
- The PDF has 14 pages, and references begin on page 10, so the body is 9 pages, within the 8–9 page limit.

**One check I couldn't run:** `compile_latex.sh` doesn't finish here. `pdftotext` isn't installed, so I supplied a substitute, and the script then rejects a system TeX config file (`/usr/share/texmf/web2c/texmf.cnf`) as outside its approved locations. That stops it before BibTeX and before its check that the Supplementary Information is rendered. I built the PDF by hand with `pdflatex` and `bibtex` instead, so the final compile in the main pipeline still needs to run that check.

The figure-manifest validator also can't run here because it depends on pipeline code that isn't installed. I didn't change any figures.
